'''II.''' ''n.'' A member of the ''Tubulariæ.'' {{smaller|The tubularian polyps form an extensive series, by some authors divided into many families. Some of them resemble slender-stemmed composite flowers, as a dandelion, for example. In the usual forms the hydranth is flower-like and borne upon the end of a slender stalk (hydrocaul), several of which may unite below into a root-like part (hydrorhiza). The hydranth bears the gonophores upon stalks (blastostyles); these may be permanently attached (sporosacs), or may become detached and float off as free medusoids. Both hydranths and gonophores are naked (gymnoblastic or athecate).}}

'''tubularidan''' (tū-bụ̄-lar′i-da̤n), ''a.'' and ''n.'' Same as ''tubularian.''

